From 3d6582565b65abc0776f5607bd01e196283f9758 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dotheroes <dotheroes@localhost>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 07:34:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 45/50] spu_workload: async SPU tasks on kept worker threads

A cut grass tuft in 3D Dot Game Heroes throws debris that Bullet
simulates on SPU: per step one BulletCD task and ~70 BulletSolver tasks,
~2,000 a second while it settles, created on the main thread, which then
polls for their results. Each task got a host thread of its own, created
at dispatch: ~116 us before it started and 361 us to run cold, and the
frame rate fell under 40 (6-40 fps in the owner's play session).

Now a task goes to an idle worker, and a new worker is made only when
none is idle, so as many tasks run at once as before (a task may block in
WAIT_SIGNAL until another signals it) and the threads are kept. Running
tasks on the dispatching thread instead hangs the game: they wait on one
another. Image 22 (the cri task) still gets a thread per task: its EXIT
handling counts calls per thread. SPU_POOL=0 is the old way.

tests/scripts/grass_cut.drive: starting a task 116 -> 4 us, running one
361 -> 122 us, the burst at 51-56 fps instead of ~40, worst frame 34 ms
instead of 110.

Also SPU_TASKPROF=<secs>, where an async task's wall time goes per image
(start, setup, run, rest), and the per-task log lines (dispatch HIT,
taskset context built, RETURNED) down to the first 48, SPU_TASK_LOG=1 for
all: three lines a task, one flushed, were 65 MB in a three-minute
session.
